y lines.**  \n   Starting from the three lines above, we may introduce any number of additional distinct lines with slopes different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); each such line is sunny. Because the covering condition is already satisfied by the three special lines, adding extra lines does not destroy it. Hence for any integer \\(t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le n-3\\) we can take the three non‑sunny lines together with \\(t\\) arbitrary sunny lines to obtain a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k=t\\) sunny lines.\n\nThe construction shows that every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\) is attainable. The cases \\(k=n-2,\\;n-1,\\;n\\) would require at most one, two or zero non‑sunny lines, respectively; whether such configurations exist for all \\(n\\) remains open.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_{n}=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nWe must find, for a given \\(n\\ge 3\\), all integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines \\(\\ell_{1},\\dots ,\\ell_{n}\\) such that  \n\n1. \\(\\displaystyle\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}\\ell_{i}\\supseteq S_{n}\\);  \n\n2. exactly \\(k\\) of the \\(\\ell_{i}\\) are *sunny*, i.e. their slopes are different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Three non‑sunny lines cover \\(S_{n}\\))\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_{x}&\\colon y=1,\\\\[2pt]\nL_{y}&\\colon x=1,\\\\[2pt]\nL_{d}&\\colon x+y=n+1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThen \\(L_{x}\\) is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, \\(L_{y}\\) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis and \\(L_{d}\\) is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\). Moreover  \n\n\\[\nS_{n}\\subseteq L_{x}\\cup L_{y}\\cup L_{d}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\).\n\n* If \\(a=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\in L_{y}\\).  \n* If \\(b=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\in L_{x}\\).  \n* Otherwise \\(a\\ge 2\\) and \\(b\\ge 2\\). From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \