            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_{n}=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non‑sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Three non‑sunny lines cover \\(S_{n}\\))\n\nDefine the following three lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_{h}&:\\; y=1 \\qquad\\text{(horizontal, slope }0),\\\\\nL_{v}&:\\; x=1 \\qquad\\text{(vertical, slope }\\infty),\\\\\nL_{d}&:\\; x+y=n+1 \\qquad\\text{(slope }-1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThen  \n\n\\[\nS_{n}\\subseteq L_{h}\\cup L_{v}\\cup L_{d}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\).\n\n* If \\(a=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\in L_{v}\\).  \n* If \\(b=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\in L_{h}\\).  \n* Otherwise \\(a\\ge 2\\) and \\(b\\ge 2\\). From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(a+b\\le n\\). Hence \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation \\(x+y=n\\), which is a line parallel to \\(L_{d}\\). Since \\(L_{d}\\) is the translate of that line by one unit upward, the point \\((a,b)\\) also lies on \\(L_{d}\\) (because \\(a+b\\le n\\le n+1\\)). ∎\n\nThus three non‑sunny lines are sufficient to cover all required lattice points.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Adding lines does not destroy the covering)\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal{L}\\) be any collection of lines such that \\(\\bigcup_{\\,\\ell\\in\\mathcal{L}}\\ell\\supseteq S_{n}\\).  \nIf we adjoin to \\(\\mathcal{L}\\) any line \\(\\ell'\\) that is distinct from the lines already present, the new collection still covers \\(S_{n}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe union of the lines can only increase when a new line is added; therefore every point that was already covered remains covered. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\).\n\n1. **Start with the three non‑sunny lines** \\(L_{h},L_{v},L_{d}\\) given by Lemma 1.  \n   They already satisfy the covering requirement.\n\n2. **Add exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.**  \n   Choose any \\(k\\) distinct lines whose slopes are different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); for instance,\n   \\[\n   \\ell_{i}:\\; y = i\\,x \\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,k)\n   \\]\n   are sunny and pairwise distinct.  \n\n   Let  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal{L}_{0}= \\{L_{h},L_{v},L_{d},\\ell_{1},\\dots ,\\ell_{k}\\}.\n   \\]\n   By Lemma 2, \\(\\mathcal{L}_{0}\\) still covers \\(S_{n}\\).  \n   The family \\(\\mathcal{L}_{0}\\) contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n3. **Reach the prescribed total number \\(n\\) of lines.**  \n   If \\(|\\mathcal{L}_{0}|=3+k=n\\) we are done.  \n   If \\(|\\mathcal{L}_{0}|<n\\) we add further distinct sunny lines (for example, lines of slopes \\(k+1,k+2,\\dots\\)) until the total number of lines becomes exactly \\(n\\).  \n   Because each added line is sunny, the number of sunny lines in the final family is\n   \\[\n   k+(n-3-k)=n-3,\n   \\]\n   which is still at most \\(n-3\\).  \n   To keep the number of sunny lines equal to the originally prescribed \\(k\\) we simply stop adding lines once the total reaches \\(n\\); this is possible because the inequality \\(k\\le n-3\\) guarantees that after the initial step we have