 a fixed \\(c\\) there is exactly one ordered pair of positive integers with sum \\(c\\); hence again at most one point of \\(T_n\\) lies on \\(L\\).\n\nConsequently each non‑sunny line covers at most one element of \\(T_n\\).\n\nA line of any slope can intersect the triangular region \\(\\{x>0,\\;y>0,\\;x+y\\le n+1\\}\\) in at most one lattice point on each diagonal \\(x+y=d\\) (\\(d=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\)). Hence any line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nIf a configuration contains \\(s\\) non‑sunny lines and \\(n-s\\) sunny lines, the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\ns\\cdot1+(n-s)\\cdot n = n^2-s(n-1).\n\\]\n\nSince all \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) points must be covered, we need  \n\n\\[\nn^2-s(n-1)\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\ns\\le\\frac{2n^2-n(n+1)}{2(n-1)}=\\frac{n}{n-1}\\le3 .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(s\\le3\\), i.e. at most three lines are non‑sunny, and therefore  \n\n\\[\nk=n-s\\ge n-3 .\n\\qquad\\Box\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \n\n*For each \\(s\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(s\\) non‑sunny lines.*\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n**Step 1 – a sunny covering family.**  \nChoose a slope different from the three forbidden ones; we take \\(m=\\frac12\\).  \nDefine, for \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\),\n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y = \\frac12 x + \\frac{i}{2}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(S_i\\) is sunny (its slope is \\(\\frac12\\neq0,-1\\) and it is not vertical).  \n\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) set \\(i=a+b-1\\) (so \\(i\\in\\{0,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\)). Then\n\n\\[\nb = \\frac12 a + \\frac{i}{2}\n\\iff 2b-a = i = a+b-1,\n\\]\n\nwhich holds identically. Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(S_{i+1}\\).  \nThus the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_n\\}\\) cover all points of \\(T_n\\). This gives a configuration with \\(s=0\\) (i.e. \\(k=n\\)).\n\n**Step 2 – replacing the first few lines.**  \n\nThe line \\(S_1\\) is the unique line that contains the point \\((1,1)\\) (the only element of \\(T_n\\) with \\(a+b=2\\)).  \nThe line \\(S_2\\) is the unique line that contains the two points \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) (the points with \\(a+b=3\\)).  \nThe line \\(S_3\\) is the unique line that contains the three points \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\) (the points with \\(a+b=4\\)).  \nBecause each of these lines is the *only* line of slope \\(\\frac12\\) passing through the corresponding diagonal, we may replace them by any non‑sunny lines that still pass through the same points.\n\n- **\\(s=1\\).**  \n  Replace \\(S_1\\) by the vertical line \\(V_1:x=1\\).  \n  The point \\((1,1)\\) lies on \\(V_1\\); all other points of \\(T_n\\) have \\(a+b\\ge3\\) and remain on \\(S_2,\\dots ,S_n\\).  \n  Exactly one line is non‑sunny.\n\n- **\\(s=2\\).**  \n  Replace \\(S_1\\) by \\(V_1:x=1\\) and replace \\(S_2\\) by the horizontal line \\(H_1:y=1\\).  \n  The points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) are on \\(V_1\\) and \\(H_1\\) respectively, while \\((1,2)\\) also lies on \\(V_1\\).  \n  All points with \\(a+b\\ge4\\) are still covered by \\(S_3,\\dots ,S_n\\).  \n  Thus we have exactly two non‑sunny lines.\n\n- **\\(s=3\\).**  \n  Replace \\(S_1, S_2, S_3\\) respectively by  \n\n  \\[\n  V_1:x=1,\\qquad H_1:y=1,\\qquad D_2:x+y=2 .\n  \\]  \n\n  The three non‑sunny lines cover the points \\((1,1), (1,2), (2,1)\\) (the points with \\(a+b\\le4\\)).  \n  Every remaining point of \\(T_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\ge5\\) and therefore lies on one of the sunny lines \\(S_4,\\dots ,S_n\\).  \n  Hence we obtain a configuration with exactly three non‑sunny lines.\n\nIn each of the three constructions the total number of lines is still \\(n\\), all lines are distinct, and the covering condition is fulfilled. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final Answer\n\nFrom Lemma 1 we have the necessary condition \\(k\\ge n-3\\).  \nLemma 2 shows that every 