l T_{n}\\) whose abscissa is at most \\(n-1\\) lie on one of\nthe \\(V_{i}\\).  \nThe only point of \\(\\mathcal T_{n}\\) not covered by the verticals is\n\\((n,1)\\).  Choose any line through \\((n,1)\\) whose slope is different from\n\\(0,\\infty,-1\\); for definiteness let  \n\n\\[\nS:\\;y-1=2\\,(x-n).\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(2\\) guarantees that \\(S\\) is not parallel to any of the three\nforbidden directions, so \\(S\\) is sunny.  \nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{V_{1},V_{2},\\dots ,V_{n-1},S\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly one of which is sunny, and it covers\nevery point of \\(\\mathcal T_{n}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  No configuration with \\(k\\ge 2\\) exists\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there is a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\)\ndistinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing at least two\nsunny lines.  Let \\(S_{1},S_{2}\\) be two distinct sunny lines of \\(\\mathcal\nL\\).\n\nA sunny line is **not** parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis; consequently it meets each\nvertical line in at most one point.  In particular, for a fixed abscissa\n\\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) the line \\(S_{i}\\) can contain at most one point of the\nvertical column \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid b\\ge 1\\}\\).\n\nConsider the column \\(x=a\\) that contains the point of \\(S_{1}\\) with the\nsmallest abscissa; denote this abscissa by \\(a_{1}\\).  The column\n\\(x=a_{1}\\) contains the points  \n\n\\[\n(a_{1},1),\\;(a_{1},2),\\dots ,\\bigl(a_{1},\\,n+1-a_{1}\\bigr)\n\\]\n\nall belonging to \\(\\mathcal T_{n}\\).  The line \\(S_{1}\\) can cover at most one\nof them.  The remaining \\(n-a_{1}\\) points of this column must be covered by\nother lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\).  Any line that is not vertical can intersect the\ncolumn in at most one point, so to cover those \\(n-a_{1}\\) points we need at\nleast \\(n-a_{1}\\) distinct lines that are either vertical or parallel to\n\\(x+y=0\\).\n\nExactly the same reasoning applied to the column that contains a point of\n\\(S_{2}\\) (let its abscissa be \\(a_{2}\\)) shows that at least \\(n-a_{2}\\) further\nlines are required.\n\nSince the two sunny lines are distinct, their abscissae satisfy\n\\(a_{1}\\neq a_{2}\\); without loss of generality \\(a_{1}\\le a_{2}\\).  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\bigl(n-a_{1}\\bigr)+\\bigl(n-a_{2}\\bigr)\\ge (n-a_{1})+(n-a_{1})=2n-2a_{1}\\ge n+1,\n\\]\n\nbecause \\(a_{1}\\le n-1\\).  Adding the two sunny lines themselves, the total\nnumber of lines needed is at least\n\n\\[\n2\\;+\\;(n-a_{1})\\;+\\;(n-a_{2})\\;\\ge\\; n+2,\n\\]\n\nwhich exceeds the allowed number \\(n\\).  This contradiction shows that a\nfamily with two or more sunny lines cannot exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Conclusion\n\nThe constructions of §1 demonstrate that configurations exist for\n\\(k=0\\) and for \\(k=1\\).  The impossibility proof of §2 shows that no\nconfiguration can exist for any \\(k\\ge 2\\).  Therefore the set of admissible\nvalues of \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1\\}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
