            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal T_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad\nC_{a}= \\{(a,b)\\mid b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\\;(1\\le a\\le n).\n\\]\n\nThus \\(|C_{a}|=n+1-a\\) and \\(\\displaystyle |\\mathcal T_{n}|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1  \nIf a line is not parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, then it meets each column \\(C_{a}\\)\nin at most one point.\n\n*Proof.*  A column \\(C_{a}\\) is the vertical line \\(x=a\\) restricted to the\nrelevant range of \\(y\\).  A non‑vertical line has an equation \\(y=mx+b\\) with\nfinite slope \\(m\\); substituting \\(x=a\\) gives a unique value of \\(y\\).  Hence at\nmost one point of the column lies on the line. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A necessary lower bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nA sunny line is, by definition, not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis,\nnor to the line \\(x+y=0\\); in particular it is not vertical, so Lemma 1 applies.\n\nThe column \\(C_{1}\\) contains exactly \\(n\\) points; a single vertical line\n\\(x=1\\) covers all of them.  All remaining points number\n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal T_{n}\\setminus C_{1}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-n=\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nEach sunny line can contain at most one point from each of the columns\n\\(C_{2},\\dots ,C_{n}\\); therefore it can cover at most \\(n-1\\) of the points\noutside \\(C_{1}\\).  If \\(k\\) denotes the number of sunny lines, we must have\n\n\\[\nk\\,(n-1)\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n-1)}{2},\n\\qquad\\text{hence}\\qquad\nk\\;\\ge\\;\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  An upper bound\n\nObviously we cannot have more than \\(n\\) distinct lines, so \\(k\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Construction for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nFix such a \\(k\\) and set \\(v=n-k\\) (the number of vertical lines we shall use).\n\n*Vertical lines.*  \nFor each \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,v\\) take the line  \n\n\\[\nV_{i}:\\;x=i .\n\\]\n\nThese \\(v\\) lines cover completely the columns \\(C_{1},C_{2},\\dots ,C_{v}\\).\n\n*Points that still have to be covered.*  \nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P:=\\bigcup_{a=v+1}^{n}C_{a}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of points lying in the remaining columns.  Its cardinality is\n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal P|=\\sum_{a=v+1}^{n}(n+1-a)\n          =\\frac{(n-v)(n-v+1)}{2}\n          =\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}\\le k\\,(n-1),\n\\]\n\nthe last inequality following from \\(k\\ge\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\).\n\n*Distribution of \\(\\mathcal P\\) among sunny lines.*  \nBecause each sunny line can meet a given column in at most one point,\nwe may assign to each sunny line at most one point from each column\n\\(C_{v+1},\\dots ,C_{n}\\).  Since there are \\(k\\) sunny lines and at most\n\\(n-v=k\\) columns, we can distribute all points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) so that\nno line receives two points from the same column.  This is always possible:\nprocess the columns one by one, and in each column give its points to\ndifferent sunny lines that have not yet received a point from that column.\n\n*Definition of the sunny lines.*  \nFix a sunny line \\(S\\) and let \\(Q_{1},\\dots ,Q_{t}\\) be the points assigned to\nit (by construction, the abscissae of the \\(Q_{j}\\) are distinct).  The unique\nline passing through all these points has equation \\(y=mx+b\\) with a finite\nslope \\(m\\).  Because the points have distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates, the slope\ncannot be \\(0\\) (otherwise all points would have the same \\(y\\)-coordinate,\nwhich is impossible) and cannot be \\(-1\\) (that would make the line parallel\nto \\(x+y=0\\)).  Moreover the line is not vertical.  Hence each such line is\nsunny.  By a tiny perturbation of the slope we can avoid the forbidden slopes\n\\(0,\\infty,-1\\) while keeping the line through the prescribed points; the\nperturbed line remains distinct from all others because the sets of points\nassigned to different sunny lines are disjoint.\n\nThus we obtain exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines, together with the \\(v=n-k\\)\nvertical lines, for a total of \\(n\\) distinct lines.  By construction every\npoint of \\(\\mathcal T_{n}\\) lies on at least one of these lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Final statement\n\nFrom (2) we have the necessary condition \\(k\\ge\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\); the\nconstruction shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\le k\\le n\\)\nis sufficient.  Consequently the set of all admissible numbers of sunny lines\nis\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\; \\{\\,\\lceil n/2\\rceil,\\;\\lceil n/2\\rceil+1,\\dots ,\\,n\\}\\; } .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
