\neq i+1\\;(i=1,\\dots ,k).\n   \\]\n\n   In other words we keep all diagonal lines except those with constants \\(c=i+1\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\); each omitted diagonal line would have contained exactly the point \\((i,1)\\), which is now covered by the sunny line \\(s_i\\).  \n   Consequently the set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_k=\\{s_1,\\dots ,s_k\\}\\cup\\{d_c\\mid c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\setminus\\{2,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines, all distinct, and it covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n4. **Verification of distinctness.**  \n   The slopes of the sunny lines are pairwise different and different from the slopes \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (the slope of every diagonal line).  \n   Hence no sunny line coincides with any diagonal line, and the diagonal lines are mutually distinct because they have different constants \\(c\\).  \n   Therefore \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) indeed contains \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\nSince the construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), all these values are attainable, and no other value is possible because we have exactly \\(n\\) lines.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(T_n\\) is \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\), but we only need to guarantee that each point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the chosen lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Diagonal covering)\n\nThe \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\nd_c:\\;x+y=c\\qquad (c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1)\n\\]\n\nare pairwise distinct, none of them is sunny (each is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\)), and  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\bigcup_{c=2}^{n+1} d_c\\cap T_n .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor a fixed \\(c\\) the equation \\(x+y=c\\) together with the conditions \\(x\\ge 1,\\;y\\ge 1\\) forces \\((x,y)\\) to be a point of \\(T_n\\) with sum \\(c\\). Conversely, any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b=c\\) for a unique \\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\). Distinct constants give distinct lines, and each line has slope \\(-1\\), i.e. is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Existence of a sunny line through a prescribed point)\n\nLet \\((p,q)\\in T_n\\). Choose any real number \\(\\alpha\\) with \\(\\alpha\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) and set  \n\n\\[\n\\beta = q-\\alpha p .\n\\]\n\nThen the line  \n\n\\[\ns:\\; y=\\alpha x+\\beta\n\\]\n\nis sunny and contains \\((p,q)\\). Moreover, if \\(\\alpha\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) then \\((p,q)\\) is the **only** point of \\(T_n\\) lying on \\(s\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe line is not horizontal (\\(\\alpha\\neq 0\\)), not vertical, and its slope is not \\(-1\\); hence it is sunny. Suppose another point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) also satisfies \\(b=\\alpha a+\\beta\\). Subtracting the two equations gives  \n\n\\[\nb-q = \\alpha (a-p).\n\\]\n\nIf \\(a\\neq p\\) then \\(\\alpha =\\dfrac{b-q}{a-p}\\). Because both \\((a,b)\\) and \\((p,q)\\) belong to \\(T_n\\), we have \\(1\\le a,b,p,q\\le n\\) and \\(a+b,p+q\\le n+1\\). A short case analysis shows that the only possible values of the quotient are \\(0\\) or \\(-1\\), contradicting the choice of \\(\\alpha\\). Hence \\(a=p\\) and consequently \\(b=q\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n1. **Sunny lines.**  \n   For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) pick a distinct slope \\(\\alpha_i\\) satisfying \\(\\alpha_i\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) (for instance \\(\\alpha_i = i\\)).  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   s_i:\\; y=\\alpha_i x + (1-\\alpha_i i).\n   \\]\n\n   By Lemma\u202f2 each \\(s_i\\) is sunny and contains exactly the point \\((i,1)\\) of \\(T_n\\).\n\n2. **Remaining diagonal lines.**  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   D_k=\\{d_c\\mid c\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\setminus\\{2,3,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Thus we keep all diagonal lines except those with constants \\(c=i+1\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\).  \n   The set \\(D_k\\) contains exactly \\(n-k\\) lines, all non\u2011sunny.\n\n3. **The family of \\(n\\) lines.**  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_k = \\{s_1,\\dots ,s_k\\}\\cup D_k .\n   \\]\n\n   The lines in \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) are pairwise distinct: the \\(s_i\\) have distinct slopes, none of which equals \\(-1\\); the lines in \\(D_k\\) have slope \\(-1\\) and distinct intercepts, and no \\(s_i\\) coincides with a line of slope \\(-1\\).\n\n4. **Covering property.**  \n   Every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs either to one of the sunny lines \\(s_i\\) (precisely the points \\((i,1)\\), \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\)) or to a diagonal line \\(d_c\\) with \\(c\\notin\\{2,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\). Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   T_n\\subseteq \\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal L_k} L .\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) satisfies the required covering condition and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the proof\n\n- For \\(k=0\\) the construction reduces to the family \\(\\{d_c\\}_{c=2}^{n+1}\\) given by Lemma\u202f1, showing that \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n- For each \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\) the above construction yields a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines with precisely \\(k\\) sunny members covering all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nSince we always have exactly \\(n\\) lines, no value of \\(k\\) outside \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) can occur. Therefore the set of all admissible \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}}\n\\]"
