ation of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers all points of \\(T_n\\) and contains \\(s\\) sunny lines. Let the remaining \\(n-s\\) lines be non‑sunny. By Lemma 1 the total number of incidences (counted with multiplicity) is at most  \n\n\\[\n(n-s)\\cdot n+s\\cdot (n-1)=n^2-s .\n\\]\n\nSince each point of \\(T_n\\) must be incident to at least one line, we need  \n\n\\[\nn^2-s\\ge |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nRearranging gives  \n\n\\[\ns\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThus **any admissible configuration satisfies \\(k\\le n-2\\).**  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Existence for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nWe construct, for each such \\(k\\), a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n#### 2.1. A base covering with no sunny lines\n\nDefine the following three non‑sunny lines:\n\n\\[\nL_1:\\;x=1,\\qquad\nL_2:\\;y=1,\\qquad\nL_3:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n-1\\) add the horizontal line  \n\n\\[\nH_j:\\;y=j .\n\\]\n\nThe total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\n3+(n-2)=n .\n\\]\n\n*Coverage proof.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n* If \\(b=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\in L_2\\).  \n* If \\(a=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\in L_1\\).  \n* If \\(a+b=n+1\\) then \\((a,b)\\in L_3\\).  \n* Otherwise \\(a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2\\) and \\(a+b\\le n\\); consequently \\(b\\) lies in \\(\\{2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) and \\((a,b)\\in H_b\\).\n\nHence every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the \\(n\\) lines. All lines are non‑sunny, so this base configuration realises \\(k=0\\).\n\n#### 2.2. Introducing sunny lines\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nWe will replace exactly \\(k\\) of the horizontal lines \\(H_j\\) by sunny lines, keeping the total number of lines equal to \\(n\\).\n\n1. **Keep the two non‑sunny lines** \\(L_1\\) and \\(L_3\\). They will remain in every construction.\n\n2. **Select \\(k\\) distinct points** of \\(T_n\\) that 