            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nWe must determine for which non‑negative integers \\(k\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines in the plane such that  \n\n1. every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the lines, and  \n2. exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are *sunny* (i.e. not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Diagonal covering)\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nd_c:\\;x+y=c .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) then \\(c:=a+b\\) satisfies \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\), so \\((a,b)\\in d_c\\). Conversely, any point of \\(d_c\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) automatically satisfies \\(a+b=c\\le n+1\\), hence belongs to \\(T_n\\).  \nThe lines \\(d_c\\) are pairwise distinct and each has slope \\(-1\\); therefore none of them is sunny. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Maximum number of points of \\(T_n\\) on a sunny line)\n\nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line.\n\n*Case 1 – irrational slope.*  \nA line with irrational slope contains at most one lattice point; consequently it contains at most one point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Case 2 – rational slope.*  \nWrite the slope as \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{p}{q}\\) with integers \\(p,q\\) such that \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\) and \\(q>0\\).  \nIf \\((a,b)\\) and \\((a',b')\\) are two distinct lattice points of \\(L\\) then \\((a'-a,b'-b)=(q,p)\\). Hence the sum of the coordinates changes by  \n\n\\[\n(a'+b')-(a+b)=p+q .\n\\]\n\nBecause we are interested in the *absolute* change of the sum, we consider  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta:=|p|+|q|.\n\\]\n\nThe vector \\((q,p)\\) moves a lattice point to another lattice point, and the quantity \\(a+b\\) changes by either \\(+\\Delta\\) or \\(-\\Delta\\) depending on the direction.  \n\nA sunny line is **not** parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis (\\(p=0\\)), not parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis (\\(q=0\\)), and not parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) (\\(p=-q\\)). Consequently \\(\\Delta\\ge2\\).\n\nChoose a point \\((a_0,b_0)\\in L\\cap T_n\\) with minimal value of \\(a+b\\). Moving in the direction that *increases* \\(a+b\\) we obtain a sequence of lattice points of \\(L\\) whose sums are  \n\n\\[\na_0+b_0,\\;a_0+b_0+\\Delta,\\;a_0+b_0+2\\Delta,\\dots\n\\]\n\nAll of them must stay \\(\\le n+1\\) to belong to \\(T_n\\). Therefore the number of points of \\(T_n\\) on \\(L\\) is at most  \n\n\\[\n1+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-(a_0+b_0)}{\\Delta}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\le 1+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nThus **every sunny line contains at most \\(\\displaystyle\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(T_n\\).** ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Impossibility of \\(k\\ge1\\))\n\nThe set \\(T_n\\) has  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{c=2}^{n+1}(c-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\npoints.\n\nAssume we have \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  \nThe remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are non‑sunny; the only non‑sunny lines that can meet points of \\(T_n\\) are the diagonals \\(d_c\\) (they are the only lines parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)). Because the lines are distinct, at most one diagonal can be omitted for each sunny line, i.e. we keep at most \\(n-k\\) diagonals.\n\nTo maximise the number of points covered by the non‑sunny lines we keep the *largest* diagonals, namely  \n\n\\[\nd_{k+2},d_{k+3},\\dots ,d_{n+1}.\n\\]\n\nTheir contribution is, using (3.1),\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{c=k+2}^{n+1}(c-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{3.2}\n\\]\n\nBy Lemma 2 the \\(k\\) sunny lines together cover at most  \n\n\\[\nk\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\tag{3.3}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(T_n\\).\n\nAdding (3.2) and (3.3) we obtain an upper bound for the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) that can be covered:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}+k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{3.4}\n\\]\n\nSince every point of \\(T_n\\) must be covered, (3.4) must be at least \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\). Cancelling the common term yields\n\n\\[\n-\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}+k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\ge0\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\nk\\Bigl(\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor-\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr)\\ge0 .\n\\tag{3.5}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(k\\ge0\\), inequality (3.5) forces  \n\n\\[\nk\\le 2\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor-1 .\n\\tag{3.6}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(k\\ge1\\) then the omitted diagonals are \\(d_{2},d_{3},\\dots ,d_{k+1}\\); they contain  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{c=2}^{k+1}(c-1)=\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}\n\\tag{3.7}\n\\]\n\ndistinct points of \\(T_n\\).  \nThe sunny lines can cover at most the quantity in (3.3). A direct comparison shows that for every integer \\(k\\ge1\\)\n\n\\[\n\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}>k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor ,\n\\]\n\nbecause \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\le n\\) and the left‑hand side grows quadratically in \\(k\\) while the right‑hand side grows only linearly. Hence the sunny lines cannot compensate for all points lost by omitting the \\(k\\) smallest diagonals, and some point of \\(T_n\\) would