or\n   \\tag{4.2}\n   \\]\n\n   points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   Adding (4.1) and (4.2) we obtain an upper bound for the total number of points that can be covered:\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}+k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{4.3}\n   \\]\n\n   Since every point of \\(T_n\\) must be covered, (4.3) must be at least \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\). Cancelling the common term yields\n\n   \\[\n   -\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}+k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\ge0\n   \\Longrightarrow\n   k\\Bigl(\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor-\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr)\\ge0 .\n   \\tag{4.4}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(k\\ge0\\), (4.4) forces  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le 2\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor-1 .\n   \\tag{4.5}\n   \\]\n\n   If \\(k\\ge1\\) the omitted diagonals are \\(d_{2},d_{3},\\dots ,d_{k+1}\\); they contain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{c=2}^{k+1}(c-1)=\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}\n   \\tag{4.6}\n   \\]\n\n   distinct points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   The sunny lines can cover at most the quantity in (4.2). For every integer \\(k\\ge1\\),\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{k(k+1)}{2}>k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor ,\n   \\]\n\n   because the left‑hand side grows quadratically in \\(k\\) while the right‑hand side grows at most linearly (recall \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\le n\\)). Hence the points lost by omitting the \\(k\\) smallest diagonals cannot all be recovered by the sunny lines, contradicting the covering requirement.  \n\n   Therefore the covering condition is impossible when \\(k\\ge1\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion**  \n   Lemma 1 shows that \\(k=0\\) can be achieved. Lemma 4 shows that no \\(k\\ge1\\) is possible. Hence the only admissible number of sunny lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{k=0}.\n   \\]"
