athcal{P}\\).  \nConsider the line  \n\n\\[\nL_{(a_0,b_0)}:\\; y-b_0=\\sqrt{2}\\,(x-a_0).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nIts slope \\(\\sqrt{2}\\) is irrational, therefore it is **sunny** (it is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\)).  \nIf \\((x,y)\\) is another lattice point on \\(L_{(a_0,b_0)}\\) then  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{y-b_0}{x-a_0}=\\sqrt{2},\n\\]\n\nwhich would make \\(\\sqrt{2}\\) rational, a contradiction. Hence \\(L_{(a_0,b_0)}\\) contains **no other integer point**, in particular no other point of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\).  \nThus \\(L_{(a_0,b_0)}\\) covers exactly the single point \\((a_0,b_0)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nChoose arbitrarily a subset  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{S}\\subset\\mathcal{P},\\qquad |\\mathcal{S}|=k .\n\\]\n\nFor each \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal{S}\\) replace the diagonal line \\(\\ell_{a+b}\\) (which originally covered \\((a,b)\\)) by the sunny line \\(L_{(a,b)}\\) defined in (4).  \n\nAfter performing the replacement for all points of \\(\\mathcal{S}\\) we obtain a collection  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}= \\bigl\\{L_{(a,b)}\\mid (a,b)\\in\\mathcal{S}\\bigr\\}\n          \\;\\cup\\;\n          \\bigl\\{\\ell_c\\mid 2\\le c\\le n+1,\\;c\\neq a+b\\text{ for any }(a,b)\\in\\mathcal{S}\\bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nProperties of \\(\\mathcal{L}\\):\n\n* **Cardinality.**  \n  The first set contributes \\(k\\) lines, the second contributes \\(n-k\\) lines; hence \\(|\\mathcal{L}|=n\\).\n\n* **Distinctness.**  \n  All \\(\\ell_c\\) are distinct by construction.  \n  Each \\(L_{(a,b)}\\) has irrational slope, so it cannot coincide with any \\(\\ell_c\\) (which have slope \\(-1\\)).  \n  Two different points of \\(\\mathcal{S}\\) give two different lines because they have different intercepts.\n\n* **Coverage.**  \n  Every point of \\(\\mathcal{S}\\) lies on its corresponding sunny line.  \n  Every point of \\(\\mathcal{P}\\setminus\\m