   * \\(n-k\\) remaining diagonal lines \\(\\ell_c\\) (with the corresponding sums not used) covering all the other \\(N-k\\) points.\n\n   The total number of lines is still \\(n\\); all are distinct (different slopes or different intercepts), and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny. Hence every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) can be achieved.\n\n5. **Impossibility of other values.**  \n   By definition a line is either sunny or not sunny, so among any collection of \\(n\\) lines the number of sunny lines cannot be negative nor exceed \\(n\\). Consequently no value of \\(k\\) outside \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) can occur.\n\nThus the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely \\(0,1,\\dots ,n\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Number of required points  \n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) the equation \\(a+b=s\\) has exactly \\(s-1\\) solutions \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\).  \nHence  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal P|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\equiv N .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A covering by non\u2011sunny lines  \n\nFor every integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_c:\\;x+y=c .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(\\ell_c\\) has slope \\(-1\\); therefore it is **not sunny**.  \nIts intersection with \\(\\mathcal P\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_c\\cap\\mathcal P=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b=c\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich contains exactly \\(c-1\\) points.  \nSince the sets \\(\\ell_c\\cap\\mathcal P\\) for distinct \\(c\\) are disjoint and  \n\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{c=2}^{n+1}\\bigl(\\ell_c\\cap\\mathcal P\\bigr)=\\mathcal P,\n\\]\n\nthe family \\(\\{\\ell_c\\}_{c=2}^{n+1}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines that together cover all points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).  \nThus the case \\(k=0\\) is realizable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Sunny lines covering a single point  \n\nFix \\((a_0,b_0)\\in\\mathcal P\\).  \nConsider  \n\n\\[\nL_{(a_0,b_0)}:\\;y-b_0=\\sqrt2\\,(x-a_0).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\sqrt2\\) is irrational, so the line is **sunny** (it is not horizontal, vertical, nor of slope \\(-1\\)).  \n\nIf a lattice point \\((x,y)\\neq(a_0,b_0)\\) lay on \\(L_{(a_0,b_0)}\\), then  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{y-b_0}{\\,x-a_0\\,}=\\sqrt2,\n\\]\n\nwhich would make \\(\\sqrt2\\) rational, a contradiction. Hence \\(L_{(a_0,b_0)}\\) contains no other integer point, in particular no other point of \\(\\mathcal P\\).  \nConsequently \\(L_{(a_0,b_0)}\\) covers exactly the single point \\((a_0,b_0)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Construction for any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be such an integer.  \nChoose a set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S=\\{(1,1),(1,2),\\dots ,(1,k)\\}\\subset\\mathcal P .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nFor each \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal S\\) the sum \\(a+b\\) equals \\(b+1\\); these sums are the distinct integers \\(2,3,\\dots ,k+1\\).  \n\nFor every \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal S\\) replace the diagonal line \\(\\ell_{a+b}\\) (which originally covered \\((a,b)\\)) by the sunny line \\(L_{(a,b)}\\) defined in (3).  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\n\\bigl\\{L_{(a,b)}\\mid (a,b)\\in\\mathcal S\\bigr\\}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\bigl\\{\\ell_c\\mid 2\\le c\\le n+1,\\;c\\notin\\{a+b\\mid (a,b)\\in\\mathcal S\\}\\bigr\\}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n*Cardinality.*  \nThe first collection contributes \\(k\\) lines, the second contributes \\(n-k\\) lines; therefore \\(|\\mathcal L|=n\\).\n\n*Distinctness.*  \nAll \\(\\ell_c\\) are distinct because they have different intercepts.  \nEach \\(L_{(a,b)}\\) has irrational slope \\(\\sqrt2\\), so it cannot coincide with any \\(\\ell_c\\) (which have slope \\(-1\\)).  \nIf \\((a,b)\\neq(a',b')\\) then the two lines \\(L_{(a,b)}\\) and \\(L_{(a',b')}\\) have different intercepts, hence are distinct.  \n\n*Coverage of \\(\\mathcal P\\).*  \nEvery point of \\(\\mathcal S\\) lies on its corresponding sunny line \\(L_{(a,b)}\\).  \nEvery point of \\(\\mathcal P\\setminus\\mathcal S\\) has sum \\(c\\) with \\(c\\notin\\{a+b\\mid (a,b)\\in\\mathcal S\\}\\); such a point lies on the diagonal line \\(\\ell_c\\), which remains in \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal L}(L\\cap\\mathcal P)=\\mathcal P .\n\\]\n\n*Number of sunny lines.*  \nExactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_{(a,b)}\\) are sunny; the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are of slope \\(-1\\) and therefore non\u2011sunny.\n\nHence for the chosen \\(k\\) we have produced \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying both required conditions, with precisely \\(k\\) of them sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Exhaustiveness of the range  \n\nBy definition a line is either sunny or not sunny, so among any collection of \\(n\\) lines the number of sunny lines is an integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\).  \nThe construction above shows that **every** integer in this interval can be realized.  \nTherefore no value of \\(k\\) outside \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is possible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two stated conditions is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
