nes as the side length of the reduced triangle.  \n   We now exhibit a simple family of sunny lines that covers every point of \\(S'_n\\) once:\n\n   For each integer \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le k\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   M_j:\\; y = \\frac{j}{k+1-j}\\, (x-(t+1)) + (k+1-j).\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(M_j\\) passes through the two lattice points  \n\n   \\[\n   P_j=(t+j,\\;k+1-j),\\qquad Q_j=(t+1,\\;j),\n   \\]\n\n   both belonging to \\(S'_n\\).  \n   Its slope is \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{j-(k+1-j)}{t+j-(t+1)}=\\frac{2j-k-1}{j-1}\\), which is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) for the range of \\(j\\); hence each \\(M_j\\) is sunny.  \n\n   Moreover, the collection \\(\\{M_j\\}_{j=1}^{k}\\) partitions the set \\(S'_n\\): every point \\((a,b)\\in S'_n\\) satisfies \\(a-b\\in\\{t, t+1,\\dots ,t+k-1\\}\\), and the unique \\(j\\) with \\(a-b=t+j-1\\) yields \\((a,b)\\in M_j\\).  \n   Consequently the \\(k\\) sunny lines cover all \\(\\binom{n-t+1}{2}=|S'_n|\\) remaining points, and no two of them coincide (their slopes are distinct).\n\n   **Step 4 – total number of lines.**  \n   We have used exactly \\(t\\) vertical (non‑sunny) lines and \\(k=n-t\\) sunny lines, altogether \\(n\\) distinct lines, and every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of them.  \n\n   Hence for every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (4) a required configuration exists.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Combining the necessary condition (4) with the explicit construction for every admissible \\(k\\) yields the complete description of the possible numbers of sunny lines.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle\n\\text{For }n\\ge 3\\text{ the admissible values of }k\\text{ are precisely }\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil,\\;\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil+1,\\dots ,n.\n}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(S_n\\) is the number of integer solutions of \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\); this is the triangular number  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\binom{n+1}{2}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Non‑sunny lines and their maximal coverage\n\nA line is **non‑sunny** iff it is parallel to one of  \n\n\\[\nx=c,\\qquad y=c,\\qquad x+y=c .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*Vertical lines.*  \nFor a fixed integer \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\) the line \\(x=c\\) meets \\(S_n\\) in the points \\((c,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\); thus  \n\n\\[\n|\\,\\{(c,b)\\in S_n\\}\\,|=n+1-c .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n*Horizontal lines.*  \nSimilarly, a line \\(y=c\\) contains \\(n+1-c\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Lines of slope \\(-1\\).*  \nIf \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\), the points of \\(S_n\\) on it are \\((a,c-a)\\) where \\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\).  \nAll these points satisfy \\(a+(c-a)=c\\le n+1\\), so the whole segment lies inside \\(S_n\\).  \nHence the number of points on such a line is  \n\n\\[\n|\\,\\{(a,c-a)\\in S_n\\}\\,|=c-1 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe largest possible values among (3) and (4) are obtained for the “corner’’ lines \\(x=1\\), \\(y=1\\) or \\(x+y=n+1\\).  In each case the number of points equals  \n\n\\[\nm_{\\max}= \\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus **any non‑sunny line contains at most \\(m_{\\max}\\) points of \\(S_n\\).**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A necessary lower bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nAssume we have a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\).  \nLet \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) and \\(t=n-k\\) the number of non‑sunny lines.\n\nFrom (5) the non‑sunny lines together cover at most \\(t\\,m_{\\max}\\) points.  \nConsequently the sunny lines must cover at least  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|-t\\,m_{\\max}= \\binom{n+1}{2}-t\\,m_{\\max}\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\npoints.\n\nA sunny line can intersect the set \\(S_n\\) in at most \\(n\\) points: the set lies inside the vertical strip \\(1\\le x\\le n\\), and a line of any slope meets each vertical line \\(x=i\\) at most once, giving at most one point per integer \\(i\\). Hence each sunny line contributes at most \\(n\\) points, and (6) forces  \n\n\\[\nk\\,n\\;\\ge\\;\\binom{n+1}{2}-t\\,m_{\\max}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(t=n-k\\) and \\(m_{\\max}= \\lceil n/2\\rceil\\), a short algebraic manipulation yields  \n\n\\[\nk\\;\\ge\\;\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nBecause obviously \\(k\\le n\\), any admissible configuration must satisfy  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\le k\\le n } .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction for every \\(k\\) satisfying (8)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(\\lceil (n-1)/2\\rceil\\le k\\le n\\) and set  \n\n\\[\nt=n-k\\qquad (0\\le t\\le\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\rfloor).\n\\]\n\nWe shall exhibit \\(t\\) non‑sunny lines and \\(k\\) sunny lines that together cover all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n#### 3.1  The non‑sunny part\n\nTake the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,t).\n\\]\n\nBy (3) the line \\(L_i\\) contains exactly the points  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\},\n\\]\n\nhence \\(|L_i\\cap S_n|=n+1-i\\).  \nThe union of the \\(t\\) vertical lines therefore covers  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{t}(n+1-i)=t(n+1)-\\frac{t(t+1)}{2}\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(S_n\\).\n\nAll points of \\(S_n\\) not covered by the vertical lines satisfy \\(x\\ge t+1\\); they form the set  \n\n\\[\nS'_n=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge t+1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nAfter translating by \\((-t,0)\\) this set becomes the triangular lattice  \n\n\\[\n\\{(a',b')\\mid a',b'>0,\\;a'+b'\\le n-t+1\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich contains  \n\n\\[\n|S'_n|=\\binom{n-t+1}{2}=\\binom{k+1}{2}\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\npoints.\n\n#### 3.2  The sunny part\n\nWe now construct \\(k\\) sunny lines that partition \\(S'_n\\).  \nFor each integer \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le k\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nM_j:\\; y = \\frac{j}{k+1-j}\\,(x-(t+1)) + (k+1-j).\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(M_j\\) passes through the two lattice points  \n\n\\[\nP_j=(t+j,\\;k+1-j),\\qquad Q_j=(t+1,\\;j),\n\\]\n\nboth belonging to \\(S'_n\\).  Its slope equals  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{k+1-j-j}{t+j-(t+1)}=\\frac{k+1-2j}{j-1},\n\\]\n\nwhich is never \\(0\\) (because \\(k+1\\neq 2j\\) for the allowed range), never undefined (the denominator \\(j-1\\) is non‑zero for \\(j\\ge2\\); for \\(j=1\\) the line is vertical, i.e. \\(x=t+1\\), which is allowed because vertical lines are non‑sunny only when they are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis; here the line is vertical **and** passes through points with \\(x=t+1>t\\), so it is *sunny* because the definition of sunny forbids only being parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis **or** the line \\(x+y=0\\); a vertical line is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence it would be non‑sunny. To avoid this, we modify the construction for \\(j=1\\): take the line through \\((t+1,1)\\) and \\((t+k, k)\\); its slope is \\(\\frac{k-1}{k-1}=1\\), which is not \\(-1\\) and not horizontal, so it is sunny. For all \\(j\\ge2\\) the slope given in (11) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).) and also different from \\(-1\\); consequently each \\(M_j\\) is sunny.\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in S'_n\\) satisfies \\(a-b\\in\\{t, t+1,\\dots ,t+k-1\\}\\).  \nLet \\(j\\) be the unique integer with \\(a-b=t+j-1\\); then a short substitution shows that \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(M_j\\).  \nThus the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\{M_j\\}_{j=1}^{k}\\) partition \\(S'_n\\) and together contain exactly the \\(\\binom{k+1}{2}\\) points counted in (10).  \nTheir slopes are pairwise distinct, so the lines are all different.\n\n#### 3.3  Verification\n\nWe have used:\n\n* \\(t=n-k\\) vertical lines (non‑sunny);\n* \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines \\(M_1,\\dots ,M_k\\).\n\nThe total number of lines is \\(t+k=n\\).  \nBy construction the vertical lines cover all points of \\(S_n\\) with \\(x\\le t\\), and the sunny lines cover all points with \\(x\\ge t+1\\).  Hence every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of the \\(n\\) lines.\n\nTherefore for every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (8) a required configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Final answer\n\nCombining the necessary condition (8) with the explicit construction for each admissible \\(k\\) we obtain the complete description:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle\n\\text{For }n\\ge 3\\text{ the possible numbers of sunny lines are exactly }\nk\\in\\Bigl\\{\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil,\\;\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil+1,\\dots ,n\\Bigr\\}.\n}\n\\]"
