\(\\mathcal F^{+}\\).  \n\\(\\mathcal F^{+}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k'\\) of them sunny, and it covers precisely the set \\(T'\\).\n\nNow consider the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S = \\{L_v,\\,L_h\\}\\cup \\mathcal F^{+}.\n\\]\n\n\\(\\mathcal S\\) contains \\(n+2\\) lines. To obtain exactly \\(n+1\\) lines we delete **one** line from \\(\\mathcal S\\) as follows:\n\n*If \\(k'\\ge 1\\)* (i.e. \\(k\\le n-2\\)), delete any sunny line of \\(\\mathcal F^{+}\\).  \nThe remaining number of sunny lines is \\(k'-1 = k\\).\n\n*If \\(k'=0\\)* (i.e. \\(k=n-1\\)), all lines of \\(\\mathcal F^{+}\\) are non‑sunny; delete any one of them.  \nThe number of sunny lines stays \\(0 = k\\).\n\nDenote the resulting family by \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1}(k)\\).  \nIt contains exactly \\(n+1\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, and it covers all points of \\(T_{n+1}\\): the two border lines cover the points with a coordinate equal to \\(1\\), while the remaining \\(n\\) lines (the reduced \\(\\mathcal F^{+}\\)) cover the points of \\(T'\\).\n\nThus the inductive step is proved.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nBy induction, for every \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Final answer\n\nCombining the necessary condition (1) with the constructive existence proved above, the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\nThis completes the solution.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as sp