t of \\(D_n\\). Since a single non‑sunny line can cover all points of \\(D_n\\), the necessity of a non‑sunny line is not established, and the bound \\(k\\le n-1\\) is unsupported.\n\n---\n\n### 3. “At least one of the two axes‑parallel lines \\(x=1\\) or \\(y=1\\) must be present …”\n\n> “Indeed, the points \\((1,b)\\) for \\(2\\le b\\le n\\) … the only way to cover them without using the vertical line \\(x=1\\) would be to place a distinct sunny line through each of them, which would already require \\(n-1\\) additional lines besides the one covering \\((1,1)\\).”\n\n**Justification Gap:**  \nThe argument is informal. A rigorous proof would need to show that any sunny line can pass through at most one of the points \\((1,b)\\) (or at most two, if the line is not vertical). The statement “distinct sunny line through each of them” is asserted without justification. Moreover, the counting “\\(n-1\\) additional lines” ignores the possibility that a single sunny line could pass through two points of the form \\((1,b)\\) if it is not vertical (e.g., a line with equation \\(y = m(x-1)+b_0\\) cannot pass through two points with the same \\(x\\)-coordinate unless it is vertical). This needs explicit clarification.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Construction – Base case \\(n=3\\)\n\n> “\\(T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \n> *\\(k=0\\):* take the three non‑sunny lines \\(x=1,\\;y=1,\\;x+y=2\\).  \n> *\\(k=1\\):* take \\(x=1,\\;y=1\\) (non‑sunny) and the sunny line \\(y=2x-1\\).  \n> *\\(k=2\\):* take the two sunny lines through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\), namely  \n> \\(\\ell_1:\\;y=2x-1,\\qquad \\ell_2:\\;y=\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac12,\\) together with the non‑sunny line \\(y=1\\).”\n\n**Justification Gap (for \\(k=2\\)):**  \nWe must verify that the three points are covered:\n\n* \\((1,1)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_1\\) (since \\(1 = 2·1 -1\\)) – OK.  \n* \\((1,2)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_2\\) (since \\(2 = \\tfrac12·1 + \\tfrac12 = 1\\) – **false**). Actually \\(\\tfrac12·1 + \\tfrac12 = 1\\), not 2. So \\((1,2)\\) is **not** on \\(\\ell_2\\). Moreover, \\((1,2)\\) is not on \\(y=1\\). Hence the claimed covering fails. Therefore the base case for \\(k=2\\) is incorrect.\n\nBecause the base case is flawed, the induction that relies on it is compromised.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Inductive step – General scheme\n\n> “Assume the statement true for some \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \n> Let \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) (the upper bound for \\(n+1\\) is \\(n\\) by (1)).”\n\n**Critical Error (mis‑alignment of bounds):**  \nThe induction assumes the upper bound for \\(n+1\\) is \\(n\\) (i.e., \\(k\\le n\\)). This follows from the previously proved bound (1), which we have already identified as invalid. Hence the induction is built on a false premise.\n\n> “Split \\(T_{n+1}\\) into three disjoint parts: … \\(B\\) (border points) and \\(T'\\) (interior).”\n\nThe decomposition itself is correct.\n\n> “The translation \\((a,b)\\mapsto (a-1,b-1)\\) maps \\(T'\\) bijectively onto \\(T_n\\).”\n\nCorrect.\n\n> “Choose an integer \\(k'\\) as follows … (2) … In both cases \\(0\\le k'\\le n-1\\), so by the induction hypothesis there exists a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) with exactly \\(k'\\) sunny lines.”\n\n**Justification Gap:**  \nThe induction hypothesis is that for **any** \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) there exists a configuration of **\\(n\\) lines**. However, the hypothesis also requires that the configuration uses exactly **\\(n\\) lines**, not \\(n+1\\). The construction here translates a configuration of \\(n\\) lines covering \\(T_n\\) to a configuration covering \\(T'\\). That part is fine, but later they add two axis‑parallel lines, obtaining **\\(n+2\\)** lines, and then delete one line to get \\(n+1\\) lines. The deletion step is not justified (see later).\n\n> “Translate every line of \\(\\mathcal F\\) by the vector \\((1,1)\\); denote the translated family by \\(\\mathcal F^{+}\\).  \n> Translation preserves parallelism, distinctness and the sunny/non‑sunny nature of each line …”\n\n**Justification Gap:**  \nWhile translation certainly preserves direction vectors (hence parallelism), a brief comment should be added that the property “not parallel to \\(x\\)-axis, \\(y\\)-axis, or \\(x+y=0\\)” is invariant under translation because those three reference lines are also translated to themselves. The author’s statement is acceptable but would benefit from a short justification.\n\n> “Now add the two axis‑parallel lines \\(L_v:x=1,\\;L_h:y=1\\).  \n> The set \\(\\mathcal S=\\{L_v,L_h\\}\\cup\\mathcal F^{+}\\) consists of \\(n+2\\) distinct lines and covers all of \\(T_{n+1}\\).”\n\n**Justification Gap:**  \nIt is true that \\(L_v\\) covers all points with first coordinate \\(1\\) and \\(L_h\\) covers all points with second coordinate \\(1\\). However, the points \\((1,1)\\) belong to both lines, and the interior points are covered by \\(\\mathcal F^{+}\\). The claim that the lines are distinct is fine (unless \\(\\mathcal F^{+}\\) already contains a line \\(x=1\\) or \\(y=1\\); this possibility is not excluded). A rigorous argument should verify that none of the translated lines coincides with \\(L_v\\) or \\(L_h\\).\n\n> “We must delete exactly one line to obtain a family of \\(n+1\\) lines. The deletion is performed according to the value of \\(k\\):”\n\n#### 5.1 Case \\(k\\le n-1\\)\n\n> “Delete **one** of the two axis‑parallel lines that is **non‑sunny** and **does not reduce** the number of sunny lines. Since both \\(L_v\\) and \\(L_h\\) are non‑sunny, we may delete either of them. After the deletion we have \\(n+1\\) lines, exactly \\(k'\\;(=k)\\) of which are sunny.”\n\n**Justification Gap / Critical Issue:**  \nRemoving one of the axis‑parallel lines eliminates coverage of an entire column or row of border points. The author assumes that the remaining axis‑parallel line together with the interior lines still covers all border points, but this is false: points of the form \\((1,b)\\