 ,n).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nAll of them are parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); hence they are non‑sunny.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(a+b=j+1\\) for a unique \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_j\\).  \nThus the family \\(\\{\\ell_j\\}_{j=1}^{n}\\) covers \\(T_n\\) and contains \\(0\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Turning a chosen diagonal line into a sunny one\n\nFix an index \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and let  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,n+1-i)\\in D_n .\n\\]\n\nDefine a border point  \n\n\\[\nQ_i=\n\\begin{cases}\n(1,i) &\\text{if }i\\neq 1,\\\\[2pt]\n(2,1) &\\text{if }i=1 .\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nBoth \\(P_i\\) and \\(Q_i\\) belong to \\(T_n\\).  \nThe line \\(s_i\\) through \\(P_i\\) and \\(Q_i\\) has slope  \n\n\\[\nm_i=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\displaystyle\\frac{i-(n+1-i)}{1-i}= -\\frac{n-2i+1}{i-1} & (i\\neq 1),\\\\[8pt]\n\\displaystyle\\frac{1-(n)}{2-1}=-(n-1) & (i=1).\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nIn every case \\(m_i\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore \\(s_i\\) is sunny.  \nMoreover \\(s_i\\) contains the diagonal point \\(P_i\\) (which previously lay on the non‑sunny line \\(\\ell_{\\,n+1-i}\\)) and also the border point \\(Q_i\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Replacing several diagonal lines simultaneously\n\nLet \\(I\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) be any subset.  \nReplace, for each \\(i\\in I\\), the non‑sunny line \\(\\ell_{\\,n+1-i}\\) by the sunny line \\(s_i\\).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_n(I)=\\Bigl(\\,\\{\\ell_j\\mid j\\notin\\{\\,n+1-i\\mid i\\in I\\,\\}\\}\\Bigr)\\cup\\{\\,s_i\\mid i\\in I\\,\\}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n*The family has exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.*  \nIndeed, the sets \\(\\{\\,\\ell_j\\mid j\\notin\\{n+1-i\\mid i\\in I\\}\\,\\}\\) and \\(\\{s_i\\mid i\\in I\\}\\) are disjoint because each \\(s_i\\) contains the unique border point \\(Q_i\\), and none of the \\(\\ell_j\\) passes through any \\(Q_i\\) (the \\(\\ell_j\\) are parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) whereas each \\(Q_i\\) has slope different from \\(-1\\) with the origin).\n\n*Coverage of \\(T_n\\).*  \nEvery point of \\(D_n\\) is some \\(P_i\\). If \\(i\\in I\\) then \\(P_i\\) lies on \\(s_i\\); otherwise \\(P_i\\) lies on the unchanged line \\(\\ell_{\\,n+1-i}\\).  \nA point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n\\) lies on a line \\(\\ell_j\\) with \\(j=a+b-1\\). If that \\(\\ell_j\\) has been removed (i.e. \\(j=n+1-i\\) for some \\(i\\in I\\)), then \\(a+b=j+1=n+2-i\\) and consequently \\((a,b)=Q_i\\), which is covered by \\(s_i\\). Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_n(I)\\).\n\n*Number of sunny lines.*  \nExactly the lines \\(s_i\\) (\\(i\\in I\\)) are sunny, so \\(\\mathcal L_n(I)\\) contains \\(|I|\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Realising every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nGiven \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), choose any subset \\(I\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) of cardinality \\(|I|=k\\) and set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_n(k):=\\mathcal L_n(I).\n\\]\n\nBy the discussion in Sections 3–4, \\(\\mathcal L_n(k)\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, covers all points of \\(T_n\\), and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Final answer\n\nThe necessary condition (5) together with the explicit construction for every integer \\(k\\) satisfying it shows that the possible numbers of sunny lines are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
