ltiple of 3 whereas \\(n\\) may be any integer; a direct substitution shows the equality never holds.”\n  * **Issue:** Critical Error – the reasoning that \\(3i=n\\) cannot occur is incorrect; for example, when \\(n=9\\) and \\(i=3\\) we have \\(3i=n\\). Moreover, the equation \\(m_i=-1\\) does have solutions (e.g., \\(i=n\\) as shown above). Hence the conclusion that \\(m_i\\neq-1\\) for all \\(i\\) is false.\n* **Location:** “If \\(\\ell_{a+b-1}\\) has not been removed, the point is still covered. If it has been removed, then \\(a+b-1=n+1-i\\) for some \\(i\\in I\\), i.e. \\(a+b=n+2-i\\). By the definition of \\(Q_i\\) we have exactly \\(Q_i=(a,b)\\); consequently the point lies on the sunny line \\(s_i\\).”\n  * **Issue:** Critical Error – the equality \\(Q_i=(a,b)\\) does **not** hold for all points with \\(a+b\\le n\\). For instance, with \\(i=n\\) the removed line is \\(\\ell_1\\); the points with \\(a+b=2\\) are \\((1,1)\\), but \\(Q_n=(1,n)\\neq(1,1)\\). Thus many points become uncovered after the replacement, invalidating the coverage claim.\n* **Location:** Overall construction in Sections 3–5.\n  * **Issue:** Critical Error – because several of the lines \\(s_i\\) are not sunny and because the replacement does not guarantee that every point of \\(T_n\\) remains covered, the argument that **any** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) can be realized is false.\n\n---\n\n###"
